DEEP[1]=('Vyāsa had offered to give the king sight so that he could watch the battle himself. Dhṛtarāṣṭra refused: he could not bear to see his own kin die. So the divine sight went to Sañjaya instead, and the whole Gītā reaches us second-hand, as a report to a blind man who asked for it.',
 'The verse is a question, and an odd one. Sañjaya has just told him Bhīṣma is down; the king already knows how things are going. Commentators hear anxiety: he hopes that on such holy ground his sons might yet turn back, or that the Pāṇḍavas might lose heart. Śaṅkara’s commentary only begins in earnest at 2.10. Some read the first word of the Gītā, <i>dharma</i>, together with the last word, <i>mama</i> (“mine”, 18.78): the whole teaching lies between dharma and “mine”.',
 'The king could have asked “what is right?” Instead he asked “what are my people doing?” When something worries us, it is worth asking which of the two questions we are really asking.')
DEEP[2]=('Droṇa had been given the Kauravas’ support and a share of land; he fights for Hastināpura out of duty and obligation. His son Aśvatthāmā is on the Kaurava side too, and his best pupil, Arjuna, stands opposite.',
 'Why Droṇa and not Bhīṣma? Some say Duryodhana wanted to stir the teacher’s old feud with Drupada; others, that he trusted Bhīṣma least of all. Either way, the scene is a picture of power anxious about loyalty. Note how Sañjaya will pair the two looks of chapter 1: Duryodhana “sees” the Pāṇḍava army (1.2) and goes to talk strategy; Arjuna “sees” his kinsmen (1.26–28) and is flooded with grief. The same word, two worlds. The Gītā will later speak of the “divine eye” (11.8) that sees truly; chapter 1 shows two people who do not yet have it.',
 'Before a hard meeting, check whom you are going to and why. Duryodhana went to the person he could press, not the person he should consult. We often do the same.')
DEEP[3]=('Droṇa had humiliated Drupada after a quarrel over a childhood promise, taking half his kingdom with Arjuna’s help. Drupada’s sacrifice then gave him two children from the fire: Dhṛṣṭadyumna, born to kill Droṇa, and Draupadī. Every name in this verse carries that history.',
 'There are two pointed words: <i>śiṣyeṇa</i>, “by your pupil”, and <i>dhīmatā</i>, “by the clever one”. Commentators read the second as a double edge: Dhṛṣṭadyumna is clever because Droṇa trained him. Duryodhana also calls the army <i>mahatī camū</i>, “a great army”, though it is the smaller one. Fear makes the other side look bigger. A teacher’s duty is to teach whoever comes sincerely, and Droṇa kept that duty even when it cost him. The Gītā later praises the giving of knowledge to the worthy (18.68–70), and Droṇa’s choice can be read in that light.',
 'Doing the right thing for someone who may later turn against us is hard, and it is still right. Droṇa’s generosity is one of the quiet virtues in a dark scene.')
DEEP[4]=('Sātyaki was one of the few Yādavas to fight for the Pāṇḍavas; the Yādava army itself fought on the Kaurava side, because Duryodhana had chosen Kṛṣṇa’s troops rather than Kṛṣṇa. Virāṭa had given his daughter Uttarā in marriage to Abhimanyu.',
 'The roll call (verses 4–6 for one side, 8–9 for the other) is a classic device of epic poetry, like the catalogue of ships in Homer. It also slows the scene down, holding the armies still so that the crisis can come. Commentators note that Duryodhana starts with the enemy’s heroes before naming his own: a sign of where his mind is. <i>Śūrāḥ maheṣvāsāḥ</i>, “heroes, great archers”, is generous praise from a foe. Fear and respect are mixed in his words. This is not a cartoon villain but a capable king who knows exactly how strong the other side is.',
 'Making an honest list of what we are up against is good. Knowing when to stop listing and start acting is better.')
DEEP[5]=('The king of Kāśī came from the royal house of the three princesses Bhīṣma once carried off from their svayaṃvara: Ambā, Ambikā and Ambālikā. Ambikā and Ambālikā became the mothers of Dhṛtarāṣṭra and Pāṇḍu. So even this ally is kin.',
 'Dhṛṣṭaketu’s presence is striking. His father Śiśupāla insulted Kṛṣṇa a hundred times and was killed by him at Yudhiṣṭhira’s great sacrifice, yet the son chose Kṛṣṇa’s side. Commentators see in such choices the pull of dharma over grudge. The title <i>nara-puṅgava</i>, “bull among men”, is a standard epic honour. Bulls stand for strength and dignity. The list works on Dhṛtarāṣṭra too: Sañjaya, by repeating Duryodhana’s words, makes the blind king hear how many good and noble people are against his sons.',
 'Sometimes people who have every reason to hold a grudge choose principle instead. Dhṛṣṭaketu is a small example of a big freedom.')
DEEP[6]=('Abhimanyu was about sixteen when he died. He had learned to break into the cakravyūha formation while still in his mother’s womb, hearing Arjuna describe it, but he never learned how to get out. His son Parīkṣit, born after the war, became the heir of the Pāṇḍavas.',
 'Calling these youths <i>mahārathāḥ</i>, great chariot-warriors, is high praise. Abhimanyu’s death on the thirteenth day breaks every rule of fair fighting: he was surrounded by several great warriors at once. It becomes one of the reasons the Pāṇḍavas later bend the rules themselves. The sons of Draupadī die in the night raid of Aśvatthāmā (Sauptika Parva), after the war is over. Readers who know the epic feel the irony of the list: the young men named as threats are the ones who will not see the end. Commentators link this to the grief Arjuna will soon express about the ruin of families (1.40–44).',
 'If a decision will fall hardest on those with the least say, give that real weight before deciding.')
DEEP[7]=('The phrase <i>saṃjñārtham</i>, “for your information” or “to name them”, is the formal tone of a briefing. Duryodhana speaks as a king to a general, though Droṇa is his teacher.',
 'Some commentators find a subtle hierarchy here: Duryodhana names his own leaders as “mine” (<i>mama sainyasya</i>). The same possessive that his father used in 1.1 appears again in the son. Droṇa does not reply. In the epic, his silence is telling: he is a man trapped by obligations. He fights for a cause he does not fully believe in, against students he loves. The Gītā will later say that acting against one’s nature brings grief (3.35, 18.47); Droṇa, a Brahmin teacher leading armies, is one example the tradition often points to.',
 'Silence can mean agreement, but also unease. When people around us go quiet, it is worth asking what they are not saying.')
DEEP[8]=('Bhūriśravas was a noble warrior whose family had an old feud with Sātyaki’s; he will be killed by Sātyaki on the fourteenth day after Arjuna cuts off his arm. Kṛpa is one of the few who survive the war.',
 'Karṇa’s place in the list is poignant. He was Kuntī’s first son, born before her marriage and set afloat in a basket. Kṛṣṇa and later Kuntī herself told him the truth before the war. He chose to stay with Duryodhana, who had befriended him when others mocked his low birth. Many readers see Karṇa as the epic’s most tragic figure: generous to a fault, loyal to a friend, and on the wrong side. Kṛpa is called <i>samitiñjaya</i>, “victor in battle”, perhaps flattery. Vikarṇa is the conscience of the Kauravas: he spoke up in the dice hall when no elder did.',
 'Gratitude to someone who helped us is beautiful. But gratitude does not oblige us to follow them into wrong. Karṇa’s story keeps that question open.')
DEEP[9]=('The Kaurava force was eleven <i>akṣauhiṇīs</i>, the Pāṇḍava seven. An akṣauhiṇī, in the epic’s count, had 21,870 chariots, as many elephants, 65,610 horses and 109,350 foot soldiers.',
 '<i>Nānā-śastra-praharaṇāḥ</i>, “with many kinds of weapons”, and <i>yuddha-viśāradāḥ</i>, “skilled in war”: Duryodhana counts arms and skill. He does not mention right or wrong. The Gītā will soon turn the question from “who is stronger?” to “what is right, and how should one act?”. Some commentators read <i>tyakta-jīvitāḥ</i> as an unwitting prophecy: nearly all of them will die. Of the eighteen akṣauhiṇīs that fought, only a handful of warriors survived. The tradition counts ten: seven on the Pāṇḍava side and three on the Kaurava side.',
 'Numbers and skill matter, but they are not the only question. Before asking “can we win?”, ask “should we be doing this?”')
DEEP[10]=('Bhīṣma was formally the Kaurava commander for the first ten days. Dhṛṣṭadyumna was the Pāṇḍava commander throughout, but Duryodhana names Bhīma, perhaps because Bhīma’s vow to kill him weighs on his mind.',
 'The verse is a famous puzzle. <i>Aparyāptam</i> can mean “unlimited” or “inadequate”; <i>paryāptam</i> “limited” or “sufficient”. Śrīdhara Svāmī and others take the boastful reading: our army is vast, theirs is small. Madhusūdana Sarasvatī and many modern readers take the fearful one: ours is not enough with a commander like Bhīṣma, who loves the Pāṇḍavas; theirs is enough with a fighter like Bhīma. Some editions differ slightly here. The ambiguity seems deliberate: Sañjaya reports exact words, and the listener must decide what was meant. It mirrors the war itself, where the larger army lost.',
 'When you can read your own words two ways, notice which one is true for you. Pride and fear often wear the same clothes.')
DEEP[11]=('Bhīṣma had told Duryodhana he would kill ten thousand Pāṇḍava soldiers a day, but would not kill the five brothers, and would not fight Śikhaṇḍī, whom he regarded as a woman. Duryodhana knew both conditions.',
 '<i>Ayaneṣu ca sarveṣu</i>, “at every point of the formation”, is a military instruction: hold your positions. The order “protect Bhīṣma” is ironic, since Bhīṣma is the greatest warrior present and hardly needs guarding. Commentators explain: the danger is Śikhaṇḍī, before whom Bhīṣma would lower his bow. So the guard is really against Bhīṣma’s own vow. Duryodhana’s plan rests on a man whose conscience is the weak spot. The Gītā will later teach that inner conflict is the real battlefield (6.5–6), and Bhīṣma shows it plainly.',
 'If a whole plan depends on someone acting against their own heart, it is fragile. Better to build on people’s real convictions.')
DEEP[12]=('Bhīṣma was Duryodhana’s great-uncle, son of King Śantanu and the river Gaṅgā. As a young man he took a terrible vow never to marry or claim the throne, so that his father could marry Satyavatī. That vow gave him his name, Bhīṣma, “the terrible one”.',
 'Sañjaya calls him <i>kuru-vṛddhaḥ pitāmahaḥ</i>, “the elder of the Kurus, the grandsire”, and <i>pratāpavān</i>, “mighty”. The lion’s roar (<i>siṃha-nāda</i>) was a warrior’s challenge. Commentators note that Bhīṣma does not reply to Duryodhana’s words at all; he answers with sound, as if to say: enough talk. Some read deep sadness in it: Bhīṣma knew that the war was wrong and had argued for peace in the court. Now he does what he promised. The tradition honours him greatly: he later teaches Yudhiṣṭhira from his bed of arrows in the Śānti and Anuśāsana Parvas, including the Viṣṇu Sahasranāma.',
 'Keeping a promise is noble. But a promise made for one good can bind us to a later wrong. Bhīṣma asks us to look at our oldest vows with fresh eyes.')
DEEP[13]=('War music in ancient India was elaborate. Conches signalled the start and end of fighting each day; drums carried commands across the field. The noise also built courage and frightened the enemy.',
 'The verse ends with <i>sa śabdas tumulo’bhavat</i>, “that sound was tumultuous”. The sentence form is simple: many instruments, one great noise. Commentators contrast this with the next verses, where the Pāṇḍava conches are named one by one with their owners. One side is a mass, the other a band of individuals, each with a story. Some also see a hint that the Kaurava sound comes from below, from drums and horns, while the Pāṇḍava sound begins with Kṛṣṇa’s divine conch. Sound matters in Indian thought: <i>oṃ</i> itself is sound (8.13, 17.23).',
 'In loud times, listen for the voice that has something to say rather than the one that is merely loud.')
DEEP[14]=('Agni gave Arjuna the bow Gāṇḍīva, two inexhaustible quivers and the chariot with white horses as thanks for help in burning the Khāṇḍava forest. Kṛṣṇa received his discus at the same time.',
 'Sañjaya calls Kṛṣṇa <i>Mādhava</i>, “husband of Mā (Lakṣmī)” or “descendant of Madhu”. Arjuna is <i>Pāṇḍava</i>, son of Pāṇḍu. The chariot is <i>mahati syandane</i>, “great chariot”, and the horses <i>śveta</i>, white. White is the colour of sattva, purity. The two blow at the same moment, a picture of the friendship that will hold the whole Gītā. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad image (Kaṭha 1.3.3–9) is not in the Gītā itself but has guided commentators for centuries. The Gītā’s own version comes later, when the senses are compared to horses that the wise must master (2.60–61, 6.34).',
 'A team where wisdom and strength ride together is hard to beat. Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna show how the wise and the capable can work as one.')
DEEP[15]=('Pañcajana was a demon in the shape of a conch who lived in the sea and had seized the son of Kṛṣṇa’s teacher Sāndīpani. Kṛṣṇa killed him, took the conch, and brought the boy back. Pāñcajanya means “from Pañcajana”.',
 'Each name is a small portrait. <i>Hṛṣīkeśa</i>, lord of the senses; <i>Dhanañjaya</i>, “winner of wealth”, because Arjuna conquered kings for Yudhiṣṭhira’s rājasūya sacrifice; <i>Vṛkodara</i>, “wolf-belly”, for Bhīma’s huge appetite; <i>bhīma-karmā</i>, “of terrible deeds”. Commentators of the Vaiṣṇava schools read the order as significant: the Lord blows first, then his devotee, then the rest. Madhusūdana notes that Kṛṣṇa’s name here, Hṛṣīkeśa, tells us who is truly in charge of the battle, as later in 11.33 where Kṛṣṇa says Arjuna is only his instrument.',
 'We each carry names given by our deeds. What would yours be? It is never too late to earn a better one.')
DEEP[16]=('Yudhiṣṭhira was the son of Kuntī by Dharma; Bhīma by Vāyu; Arjuna by Indra. Nakula and Sahadeva were sons of Mādrī, Pāṇḍu’s second wife, by the twin Aśvins. All five were called sons of Pāṇḍu.',
 'The names grow gentle toward the twins. Commentators note that Yudhiṣṭhira is called <i>kuntī-putraḥ</i>, Kuntī’s son. This reminds the blind king that Kuntī, his own sister-in-law, is mother to these men. Sañjaya keeps placing family facts in front of Dhṛtarāṣṭra. Yudhiṣṭhira is the son of Dharma, so the eldest Pāṇḍava stands for dharma itself on the field of dharma (1.1). The pattern is too neat to be chance.',
 'Truthfulness is slow strength. It does not win every game, but over time it wins trust, and trust wins more than dice.')
DEEP[17]=('Ambā had been carried off by Bhīṣma and then rejected by everyone: by the king she loved, and by Bhīṣma, bound by his vow. She did severe austerities and was promised by Śiva that she would cause Bhīṣma’s death in a later birth. She was reborn as Drupada’s child and later became a man, Śikhaṇḍī.',
 'Sātyaki is called <i>aparājitaḥ</i>, “unconquered”: he survives the war. The king of Kāśī is <i>parameṣvāsaḥ</i>, “supreme archer”. Commentators often note how the Gītā’s first chapter quietly lays out cause and effect, karma, through these names. The teaching of chapter 4 on action and its fruit is foreshadowed in the plot itself. Śikhaṇḍī also invites reflection on how the epic treats identity and gender; the text accepts the change and gives Śikhaṇḍī an honoured place as a warrior. Bhīṣma, however, holds to his old view, and that becomes his undoing.',
 'Wrongs we have forgotten may be remembered by others. Making amends early is wiser than meeting them later on a battlefield.')
DEEP[18]=('Dhṛtarāṣṭra was addressed as <i>pṛthivī-pate</i>, “lord of the earth”, in courtly style. In fact his authority was hollow: Duryodhana ruled in his name.',
 '<i>Pṛthak pṛthak</i>, “each separately”, closes the catalogue: every hero sounds his own conch. The verse also mentions <i>Saubhadra</i>, Abhimanyu, with the epithet <i>mahā-bāhuḥ</i>, “mighty-armed”, the same title Kṛṣṇa will often use for Arjuna. Commentators read the moment as the last chance for peace: once all conches have sounded, war is declared. Sañjaya’s address reminds the king that he alone had the authority to call it off. In the Udyoga Parva, Vidura, Bhīṣma, Droṇa and Kṛṣṇa all urged him to do so. He loved his son too much to say no.',
 'Love that cannot say no is not always love. Sometimes the kindest thing a parent, leader or friend can do is refuse.')
DEEP[19]=('The Pāṇḍava army was smaller but full of famous warriors, and it had Kṛṣṇa. The Kauravas, for all their numbers, knew that Bhīma had sworn to kill Duryodhana and that Arjuna was nearly unbeatable.',
 'Sañjaya says the sound echoed through sky and earth, <i>nabhaś ca pṛthivīṃ caiva</i>. Some commentators read this as nature itself taking sides. Others say simply that the noise was overwhelming and frightening. The deeper reading is psychological: those who act against dharma carry hidden fear. The Gītā later describes the āsurika nature, full of anxiety and endless cares (16.11). Here we see the first sign of it in the Kaurava ranks.',
 'Our deepest fears often come from what we know we have done wrong. Setting things right is the surest way to quiet them.')
DEEP[20]=('<i>Kapi-dhvaja</i>, “he whose banner bears the monkey”, is a famous name for Arjuna. In the Vana Parva Bhīma met Hanumān, his elder brother (both are sons of Vāyu), on the way to the Gandhamādana mountain, and Hanumān promised to sit on Arjuna’s banner and roar in the battle.',
 '<i>Pravṛtte śastra-sampāte</i>: “as the clash of weapons was beginning”. Time is frozen at the last moment before battle. That is where the Gītā lives: in a pause. Commentators note that Arjuna sees (<i>dṛṣṭvā</i>) the Kauravas, the same word used of Duryodhana in 1.2. Now he will speak to Hṛṣīkeśa, the master of the senses. The name of the next speaker and the name of the listener prepare the theme: what we see and what we make of it. Vaiṣṇava readers love the presence of Hanumān here: the great devotee of Rāma guards the chariot of Kṛṣṇa.',
 'The most important moments in life often come in a pause before action. Use the pause. Arjuna’s pause became the Gītā.')
DEEP[21]=('This is Arjuna’s first speech in the Gītā. His opening words are <i>senayor ubhayor madhye</i>, “between the two armies”, a phrase that will recur at 1.24 and 2.10, framing the whole scene.',
 'The sentence spills across verses 21 and 22: Arjuna speaks in a rush, a warrior’s command. Commentators of the bhakti schools see the verse as one of the sweetest in the Gītā: the Lord accepts the role of charioteer, a lowly job, and takes orders from his friend. Kṛṣṇa will say later that he is bound by love to those who love him (9.29). <i>Acyuta</i>, “the unfallen, the unshakable”, is also a name in the Viṣṇu Sahasranāma. Arjuna does not yet know that he is about to fall, and that Acyuta will lift him.',
 'When we need to see a situation clearly, it helps to step into the middle, close enough to see faces, not just positions.')
DEEP[22]=('A warrior inspected the enemy before battle to choose his opponents. In epic fighting, great warriors often sought out their equals, and Arjuna would have wanted to see where Bhīṣma, Droṇa and Karṇa stood.',
 'The verb <i>nirīkṣ</i> means to look closely, to examine. <i>Kair mayā saha yoddhavyam</i>, “with whom I must fight”: Arjuna thinks in terms of duty, <i>yoddhavyam</i>, “must be fought”. The same idea returns at 2.18, where Kṛṣṇa says <i>tasmād yudhyasva</i>, “therefore fight”. So the Gītā begins and turns on the idea of what must be done. Madhusūdana and others suggest that Kṛṣṇa, the all-knowing, drove to the very spot where Bhīṣma and Droṇa stood, so that Arjuna’s grief would come out and could be healed.',
 'Facing what we must face, up close, is often how hidden feelings rise. That is painful, and it is also how healing starts.')
DEEP[23]=('<i>Dhārtarāṣṭrasya durbuddheḥ</i>, “of the evil-minded son of Dhṛtarāṣṭra”: Arjuna does not name Duryodhana here, only his father. It is a sharp dig.',
 'Arjuna’s words have a strong moral clarity: the Kaurava allies came <i>priya-cikīrṣavaḥ</i>, “wanting to please” a bad man. Commentators note the contrast with what follows. Within a few verses, Arjuna will see that those same people include his teachers and elders, and his clarity will dissolve. The Gītā does not let him keep a simple story; it will give him a larger one in which duty and love can both be honoured. Many modern readers find this one of the most human moments of the text: the hero is sure, then he is not.',
 'Being certain about others is easy from a distance. Up close, people are more complicated. Wisdom keeps both the principle and the person in view.')
DEEP[24]=('Sañjaya addresses Dhṛtarāṣṭra as <i>Bhārata</i>. Arjuna, too, is often called Bhārata. Both sides descend from King Bharata, after whom India (Bhārata) is named.',
 '<i>Guḍākeśa</i> can mean “master of sleep” (guḍākā, sleep + īśa, lord) or “thick-haired” (guḍā + keśa). Commentators prefer the first: Arjuna was famous for needing little sleep and for constant alertness. The pairing is deliberate. Some teachers read it inwardly: when a watchful mind (Arjuna) lets the Lord of the senses (Kṛṣṇa) drive, the chariot of the body reaches the right place. The middle ground, <i>madhye</i>, becomes the classroom of the Gītā.',
 'Stay alert, and let your best wisdom hold the reins. That combination puts you in the right place to learn.')
